//! lotus/stereo_notch.rye -- ALES173: a BAND-REJECT (NOTCH) carried into stereo -- ALES47's parallel low-pass-plus-high-pass sum run on BOTH channels with the SAME two cutoffs, the fourth rung of the stereo EQ / filter class. The
//! class now has its tone control (ALES170, one edge kept), its two-band shelf (ALES171, both bands tilted), and its band-pass (ALES172, the middle isolated); the exact complement of the band-pass is the NOTCH -- keep the edges,
//! drop the middle. It is the surgical cut a keeper reaches for: the mains hum pulled from a take, the resonant ring notched from a room, the mud scooped while the lows and the air stay whole. ALES47 built that for a mono Clip:
//! sum, in PARALLEL, a low-pass at the low cutoff (keeps everything below) and a high-pass at the high cutoff (keeps everything above), and write the sum saturated ONCE over the true wide-domain total; what neither branch keeps --
//! the middle band between the cutoffs -- is rejected. This rung carries it into stereo the way the EQ class asks -- run ALES47's proven mono `notch` on each channel with the SAME two cutoffs, so the reject band lifts on BOTH
//! speakers together.
//!
//! Like every parametric edit (ALES130's fade, ALES171's shelf, ALES172's band), a notch's cutoffs are fractions the caller NAMES, not scalars measured across the field, so the SAME two cutoffs on each channel preserve the
//! stereo image for free -- no linking, no measurement. Each channel notches its OWN content through those shared cutoffs, which is exactly right for EQ: an identical-channel (mono-in-stereo) master stays identical because the
//! same input through the same cutoffs is the same deterministic output, while a panned master shapes each side by its own spectrum. Like ALES171's shelf and unlike ALES172's band-pass, there is no carried state to lift -- ALES47's
//! notch runs a fresh low-pass and high-pass over the whole span each call, so a stereo notch is a stateless per-channel pass with nothing to thread across a seam. The band-pass's stateful complement returns to the shelf's
//! simpler shape.
//!
//! The rule, stated once: refuse BadCoeff (a low or high cutoff outside (0, 1]) and BadRange (a span past the shared length) BEFORE either channel is touched -- both cutoffs and the span validated once against the shared length
//! up front, so a refusal never notches one channel and leaves the other torn; then run ALES47's `notch` on each channel with the SAME two cutoffs. Equal cutoffs (low = high) reconstruct the input on both (the reject band has
//! zero width -- the identity); at direct current the low band keeps the constant and the high band is zero, so the notch keeps direct current, per channel.
//!
//! The crux -- four laws. THE STEREO NOTCH LAW: each channel equals ALES47's mono `notch` with the SAME two cutoffs over the same span byte for byte, proven side by side with genuinely different per-channel content -- the left
//! notched by the left's spectrum, the right by the right's, never crossed. THE ZERO-WIDTH / DIRECT-CURRENT LAW: equal cutoffs reconstruct the input byte for byte on both (the reject band of zero width the identity), and a settled
//! constant survives on both (the notch keeps direct current, per channel). THE COMPLEMENT LAW: the notch is genuinely neither the low-pass nor the high-pass alone, and it differs from ALES172's band-pass on the same cutoffs --
//! keep the edges, not the middle, per channel. THE IMAGE / BALANCE / SILENCE / ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW: an identical-channel master stays identical through the notch; an all-silent master notches to silence on both;
//! left.len == right.len after; an illegal low cutoff, an illegal high cutoff, and an out-of-range span each refuse by name with BOTH channels byte for byte untouched and still balanced; count = 0 the identity on both.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season C thread (Lotus - the creative suite) - waymark ALES - rung ALES173.
//! Design read: active-designing/date/20260815/20260815-080408_fill-ales173-lotus-stereo-notch.md
//! Stands on: lotus/notch.rye (ALES47) -- the mono band-reject run once per channel, its ToneError reused whole; lotus/tone.rye (ALES40) -- the low-pass and high-pass the notch sums, and the `precheck` sharing one
//! coefficient-and-span validation; lotus/band.rye (ALES46) -- the band-pass this notch complements; lotus/pan.rye (ALES10) -- the StereoClip (two mono Clips, left/right) and its balance invariant; lotus/timeline.rye (ALES2) -- the
//! Clip and the one true saturate the notch's sum reuses.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE -- software only, purely LOCAL. Two bounded in-process i16 Clips (left, right), each notched through ALES47's own two scratch bands, siloed to lotus/. It changes sample VALUES only, never a length; every band sum
//! runs in i64 so nothing overflows before the single clamp per channel. The cutoffs set where the reject band falls over sample INDICES, not crossovers in hertz (a real time base is a later rung). No real sample rate, no
//! anti-aliasing, no network, no keys, no funds, no real device, no real speaker.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run lotus/stereo_notch.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const timeline = @import("timeline.rye");
const pan = @import("pan.rye");
const tone = @import("tone.rye");
const notch = @import("notch.rye");
const band = @import("band.rye");

pub const stereo_notch_version = "20260815.080408";

/// The stereo notch's faults are exactly ALES47's own `ToneError` -- `BadCoeff` on an illegal cutoff (either edge) and `BadRange` on a span outside the samples -- reused by name, since carrying the notch into stereo adds no new
/// fault: it validates two shared cutoffs and one span against the shared length, then runs the same mono notch on each channel.
pub const StereoNotchError = tone.ToneError;

/// Band-reject `count` sample-PAIRS starting at `start` in BOTH channels of `sc` with the SAME two cutoffs: keep each channel's low band (a low-pass at the low cutoff) and high band (a high-pass at the high cutoff), and write
/// their sum saturated once -- ALES47's proven mono notch, run per channel. Equal cutoffs reconstruct the input on both; a settled constant survives on both (the notch keeps direct current). Validates both cutoffs and the span
/// ONCE against the shared length before either channel is mutated: refuses BadCoeff and BadRange, both channels untouched. Values only -- never the length; the two channels leave balanced.
pub fn stereo_notch(
    sc: *pan.StereoClip,
    start: u32,
    count: u32,
    low_num: i32,
    low_den: u32,
    high_num: i32,
    high_den: u32,
) StereoNotchError!void {
    // invariant: the two channels enter balanced -- the defining StereoClip invariant, the ground the shared cutoffs preserve.
    assert(sc.left.len == sc.right.len);
    // invariant: BOTH cutoffs and the span are checked against the SHARED length BEFORE any write, so a refusal never notches one channel and leaves the other torn.
    const len: u32 = sc.left.len;
    try tone.precheck(len, start, count, low_num, low_den);
    try tone.precheck(len, start, count, high_num, high_den);

    // Every check passed and holds identically for both channels (same cutoffs, same span, shared length), so each mono notch is pre-validated to succeed -- no refusal can land between the two channel edits. The SAME cutoffs on
    // each channel, each notching its own content.
    try notch.notch(&sc.left, start, count, low_num, low_den, high_num, high_den);
    try notch.notch(&sc.right, start, count, low_num, low_den, high_num, high_den);

    // invariant: the channels leave balanced -- a notch writes values only, so each channel holds its starting length and the stereo image stays aligned in time.
    assert(sc.left.len == len and sc.right.len == len);
    assert(sc.left.len <= sc.left.buf.len and sc.right.len <= sc.right.buf.len);
}

// --- selftest: THE STEREO NOTCH LAW -- each channel equals ALES47's mono notch with the SAME two cutoffs byte for byte, different per-channel content; THE ZERO-WIDTH / DIRECT-CURRENT LAW -- equal cutoffs the identity on both, a
//     settled constant surviving per channel; THE COMPLEMENT LAW -- neither low-pass nor high-pass alone, and distinct from ALES172's band-pass; THE IMAGE / BALANCE / SILENCE / ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW -- identical-channel master
//     staying identical, silence to silence, balanced, each refusal untouched, count 0 identity

fn eq(got: []const i16, want: []const i16) bool {
    if (got.len != want.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < want.len) : (i += 1) if (got[i] != want[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

fn load_stereo(sc: *pan.StereoClip, l: []const i16, r: []const i16) !void {
    try timeline.splice(&sc.left, 0, l);
    try timeline.splice(&sc.right, 0, r);
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    // 1. THE STEREO NOTCH LAW -- each channel equals ALES47's mono notch with the SAME two cutoffs, proven side by side over one span with genuinely different per-channel content: the left notched by the left's spectrum, the
    //    right by the right's, never crossed.
    {
        const ll = [_]i16{ 3000, -3000, 500, -20000, 20000, -400, 6000, -6000, 1000, 500, -900, 1100 };
        const rr = [_]i16{ -400, 1200, 500, 2000, -700, 100, -1500, 1500, 900, -800, 1200, 900 };
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &ll, &rr);
        try stereo_notch(&sc, 0, 12, 1, 3, 1, 2); // low cutoff 1/3, high cutoff 1/2 on both

        var ml = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&ml, 0, &ll);
        try notch.notch(&ml, 0, 12, 1, 3, 1, 2);
        var mr = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&mr, 0, &rr);
        try notch.notch(&mr, 0, 12, 1, 3, 1, 2);

        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), ml.samples())) return error.LeftNotchNotMono;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), mr.samples())) return error.RightNotchNotMono;
        // The two channels genuinely differ -- the left is not the right notched, proving no crossing.
        if (eq(sc.left.samples(), sc.right.samples())) return error.ChannelsDidNotDiffer;
    }

    // 2a. THE ZERO-WIDTH LAW -- equal cutoffs (low == high) are a reject band of zero width, so the low-pass and high-pass at the same coefficient reconstruct the input byte for byte on BOTH channels.
    {
        const ll = [_]i16{ 100, -200, 3000, -4000, 500, 42 };
        const rr = [_]i16{ 42, 500, -4000, 3000, -200, 100 };
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &ll, &rr);
        try stereo_notch(&sc, 0, 6, 1, 3, 1, 3); // equal cutoffs — identity
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &ll)) return error.LeftZeroWidthWrong;
        if (!eq(sc.right.samples(), &rr)) return error.RightZeroWidthWrong;
    }

    // 2b. THE DIRECT-CURRENT LAW -- a settled constant survives the notch on both channels: at direct current the low band keeps the constant and the high band is zero, so the tail equals the input exactly, per channel.
    {
        const run = [_]i16{2500} ** 40;
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &run, &run);
        try stereo_notch(&sc, 0, 40, 1, 3, 1, 2);
        if (sc.left.samples()[39] != 2500 or sc.right.samples()[39] != 2500) return error.NotchDroppedDc;
    }

    // 3. THE COMPLEMENT LAW -- the notch keeps the edges, so on a genuinely mixed signal it is neither the input (the reject band has width), nor a plain low-pass, nor a plain high-pass, and it differs from ALES172's band-pass on
    //    the same cutoffs (the band-pass keeps the middle the notch drops). Proven on one channel; the stereo notch law already ties both channels to the mono tool.
    {
        const src = [_]i16{ 1000, -800, 1200, 900, -1100, 700, 1300, -600, 1000, 500, -900, 1100 };
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &src, &src);
        try stereo_notch(&sc, 0, 12, 1, 3, 1, 2);
        if (eq(sc.left.samples(), &src)) return error.NotchIsIdentity;

        var lp = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&lp, 0, &src);
        try tone.low_pass(&lp, 0, 12, 1, 3);
        if (eq(sc.left.samples(), lp.samples())) return error.NotchEqualsLowpass;

        var hp = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&hp, 0, &src);
        try tone.high_pass(&hp, 0, 12, 1, 2);
        if (eq(sc.left.samples(), hp.samples())) return error.NotchEqualsHighpass;

        var bp = timeline.Clip{};
        try timeline.splice(&bp, 0, &src);
        var bf = band.Bandpass{ .num_low = 1, .den_low = 3, .num_high = 1, .den_high = 2 };
        try bf.run(&bp, 0, 12);
        if (eq(sc.left.samples(), bp.samples())) return error.NotchEqualsBandpass; // complement, not the band-pass
    }

    // 4a. THE IMAGE LAW -- an identical-channel (mono-in-stereo) master stays identical through the notch: the same input through the same cutoffs is the same deterministic output, so the two channels never split.
    {
        const same = [_]i16{ 900, -700, 15000, -400, 9000, -9000, 300, -300 };
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &same, &same);
        try stereo_notch(&sc, 0, 8, 1, 4, 1, 2);
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), sc.right.samples())) return error.IdentityChannelsSplit;
    }

    // 4b. THE SILENCE / BALANCE / LENGTH LAW -- an all-silent master notches to silence on both; the channels stay equal length and each holds its starting length.
    {
        var sc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&sc, &[_]i16{ 0, 0, 0, 0, 0 }, &[_]i16{ 0, 0, 0, 0, 0 });
        try stereo_notch(&sc, 0, 5, 1, 4, 1, 2);
        const zeros = [_]i16{ 0, 0, 0, 0, 0 };
        if (!eq(sc.left.samples(), &zeros) or !eq(sc.right.samples(), &zeros)) return error.SilenceWrong;

        var bc = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&bc, &[_]i16{ 8000, -8000, 200, -9000, 100 }, &[_]i16{ -400, 3000, -700, 5000, -50 });
        try stereo_notch(&bc, 0, 5, 1, 4, 1, 2);
        if (bc.left.len != bc.right.len) return error.Unbalanced;
        if (bc.left.len != 5) return error.LengthChanged;
    }

    // 4c. THE ATOMICITY LAW -- an illegal low cutoff (above one), an illegal high cutoff (zero denominator), and an out-of-range span each refuse by name with BOTH channels byte for byte untouched and still balanced.
    {
        const tl = [_]i16{ 5, -6, 7 };
        const tr = [_]i16{ -7, 6, -5 };

        var a = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&a, &tl, &tr);
        if (stereo_notch(&a, 0, 3, 3, 2, 1, 2)) |_| return error.LowOverOneSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadCoeff) return error.WrongLowOverOne;
        if (!eq(a.left.samples(), &tl) or !eq(a.right.samples(), &tr) or a.left.len != a.right.len) return error.LowRefusalMutated;

        var b = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&b, &tl, &tr);
        if (stereo_notch(&b, 0, 3, 1, 2, 1, 0)) |_| return error.HighZeroDenSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadCoeff) return error.WrongHighZeroDen;
        if (!eq(b.left.samples(), &tl) or !eq(b.right.samples(), &tr) or b.left.len != b.right.len) return error.HighRefusalMutated;

        var c = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&c, &tl, &tr);
        if (stereo_notch(&c, 1, 3, 1, 2, 1, 2)) |_| return error.BadRangeSlipped else |e| if (e != error.BadRange) return error.WrongBadRange;
        if (!eq(c.left.samples(), &tl) or !eq(c.right.samples(), &tr) or c.left.len != c.right.len) return error.RangeRefusalMutated;
    }

    // 4d. THE DEGENERATE LAW -- count = 0 (an empty span in the middle) is the identity on both channels; no pair to notch.
    {
        var noop = pan.StereoClip{};
        try load_stereo(&noop, &[_]i16{ 8000, -8000, 4000 }, &[_]i16{ -50, 60, -70 });
        try stereo_notch(&noop, 1, 0, 1, 2, 1, 2); // empty span — identity
        if (!eq(noop.left.samples(), &[_]i16{ 8000, -8000, 4000 })) return error.ZeroCountLeftChanged;
        if (!eq(noop.right.samples(), &[_]i16{ -50, 60, -70 })) return error.ZeroCountRightChanged;
    }

    print("GREEN ales-stereo-notch: a BAND-REJECT (NOTCH) CARRIED INTO STEREO — ALES47's parallel low-pass-plus-high-pass sum run on BOTH channels with the SAME two cutoffs, the fourth rung of the stereo EQ / filter class. The " ++
        "class has its tone control (ALES170), its shelf (ALES171), and its band-pass (ALES172); the exact complement of the band-pass is the notch — keep the edges, drop the middle (the mains hum pulled, the resonant ring " ++
        "notched, the mud scooped while lows and air stay whole). A notch's cutoffs are fractions the caller names, not scalars measured across the field, so the same two cutoffs on each channel preserve the stereo image for " ++
        "free (no linking, no measurement); each channel notches its own content, and like the shelf and unlike the band-pass there is no carried state to thread — the notch runs fresh low-pass and high-pass over the whole span " ++
        "each call. It validates both cutoffs and the span once against the shared length before either channel is mutated (BadCoeff, BadRange), so a refusal never notches one channel and leaves the other torn; ToneError reused " ++
        "whole. THE STEREO NOTCH LAW: each channel equals ALES47's mono notch with the same two cutoffs byte for byte, the channels genuinely differing. THE ZERO-WIDTH / DIRECT-CURRENT LAW: equal cutoffs the identity on both " ++
        "(the reject band of zero width), a settled constant surviving per channel (the notch keeps direct current). THE COMPLEMENT LAW: neither the low-pass nor the high-pass alone, and distinct from ALES172's band-pass on the " ++
        "same cutoffs — keep the edges, not the middle. THE IMAGE / BALANCE / SILENCE / ATOMICITY / DEGENERATE LAW: an identical-channel master staying identical, silence staying silence on both, left.len == right.len after, an " ++
        "illegal low cutoff / an illegal high cutoff / an out-of-range span each refusing by name with both channels untouched and balanced, count = 0 the identity on both. Purely local — two bounded i16 Clips notched through " ++
        "the proven mono notch, every band sum in i64 clamped once per channel, the cutoffs over sample indices not hertz; no real sample rate, no anti-aliasing, no network, no keys, no funds, no real speaker\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init;
    return run_selftest();
}
